# Family A16: Redis Streams negative-settlement strategies (internal/streams).
# See hack/bakeoff.sh.
PKG=./internal/streams
BENCH=BenchmarkRedisNack
REF=leavepending
AUTO_KEY=streamsnack
KIND_DIM=""
# ADR-013 collects the §9 rows that need a broker and are therefore decided in
# Part B (the plan's "Phase-0 rows that need a broker (A11-A13, A15-A17,
# A32-A35) are decided in Part B (ADR-013)").
ADR=13
TITLE="Redis Streams nack and reclaim strategies (A16)"
#
# What the four arms are, and why the family exists at all.
#
# Redis Streams has no nack. A consumer group knows two states for an entry --
# pending and acknowledged -- so "make this message deliverable again,
# optionally after a delay" has to be built out of the commands there are, and
# there are four ways to build it:
#
#   leavepending  send nothing; the mandatory XAUTOCLAIM reclaim loop
#                 redelivers once the entry has been idle for the ack
#                 deadline. Zero commands, keeps the entry's identifier and
#                 its delivery counter, and therefore keeps per-shard order
#                 and §4.5's attempt accounting. Cannot honour a delay longer
#                 than the deadline, and refuses one rather than redelivering
#                 early.
#   zsetmover     XACK and park in a sorted set scored by the deliver-at time,
#                 both in one Lua script; a mover releases it when due, also
#                 in one script. The only arm that honours an arbitrary delay.
#                 Costs a script call per nack and per release, a sorted set
#                 per shard, and a new stream identifier (so the attempt count
#                 travels in the entry).
#   readd         XACK and XADD the entry back at once, in one script. Lowest
#                 redelivery latency and the only arm whose pending-entries
#                 list does not grow; loses the message's position in its
#                 shard and cannot delay at all.
#   stop          leavepending plus a hold on the entry's shard until it is
#                 settled. The ordering-preserving arm: nothing behind the
#                 failed message moves. Pays with head-of-line blocking.
#
# The benchmark measures the PLAN, which is a pure function: no I/O, no clock,
# nothing shared between iterations. That is deliberate -- an arm's cost on a
# real server is dominated by the round trips it needs, and `cmds/op` is what
# the benchmark reports beside ns/op for exactly that reason. The behavioural
# half of §9.1's criterion is not a benchmark at all: redelivery-latency
# accuracy, PEL growth and duplicates under a crash are TestModel's subtests
# and the two fuzz targets, which run in the correctness gate.

#
# CRITERION is the benchmark configuration's decision column for this row, quoted.
# MATRIX is the IQMQ_BENCH_MATRIX grid the campaign measures the family at.
# DECIDED_AT names the benchmark-name segments the criterion is read at, and
# DECIDED_ON the metrics; `make bench-lint` lists the family's benchmarks at
# the declared grid and fails when a term or a metric is missing from the rows
# every kind has in common, which is the only data `bakeoff decide` reads.
CRITERION="leave-pending default; zset for delays (redelivery latency accuracy; PEL growth; duplicates under crash)"
# MATRIX=default is the only grid: internal/streams has no IQMQ_BENCH_MATRIX
# branch. The rows are the three §4.3 actions crossed with §10.4's batch ends,
# which is what a settlement's cost varies with here; the delay sweep is NOT a
# common dimension, because three of the four arms refuse a delay outright and
# a row measuring a refusal would decide the family on an arm's inability
# (the delay=1m rows exist for zsetmover alone).
MATRIX=default
DECIDED_AT="requeue drop deadletter delay=0 batch=1 batch=100"
DECIDED_ON="ns/op allocs/op cmds/op"
# The three criterion terms that are not benchmark metrics live in the
# correctness gate rather than in a scorecard: redelivery-latency accuracy is
# TestModel/<kind>/delay-honoured-or-refused plus TestZSetMoverHonoursTheDelay,
# duplicates-under-crash is the atomicity of each arm's Lua script, exercised
# by TestModel/<kind>/guard-refuses-a-stolen-entry and FuzzRedisReclaimer, and
# PEL growth is a property of the arm rather than a measurement (readd empties
# the list, leavepending and stop hold an entry per in-flight message,
# zsetmover moves it to a sorted set).
CRITERION_GAP="redelivery-latency accuracy, PEL growth and duplicates-under-crash=./internal/streams TestModel and FuzzRedisReclaimer (correctness gate, not benchmark metrics)"
# The family's decision rule is `rules["A16"]` in
# benchmarks/cmd/bakeoff/rules.go, and it is what `make bakeoff-dryrun` caught
# before any measurement: without a row there the campaign would have measured
# the family and then refused to decide it. The rule declares three of the four
# arms NotDefault with their reasons -- stop blocks a shard on one failing
# message, readd and zsetmover move a retried message to the end of its shard
# -- because at delay=0 every arm builds the same one command or none, so the
# medians are noise and whichever arm a run happens to favour would win. The
# dry run picked `stop` on one sample per row, which is the arm whose cost is a
# liveness property.
